  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Blueberry Blunt-horn (Melitta americana) is a solitary bee species native to New England that specializes in foraging on blueberry flowers. As a member of the Melittidae family, it is an important pollinator for wild and cultivated blueberry plants in the region.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Blunt antenna tips",
      "description": "The antennae are notably blunt or truncated at the ends, which is the defining characteristic of the genus Melitta",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Body size and coloration",
      "description": "Small bee, typically 8-10mm long, with a dark body and sparse light-colored hair",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pollen baskets",
      "description": "Females have specialized pollen-carrying structures on their hind legs adapted for collecting pollen from blueflower",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Melitta americana thrives in open woodlands, forest edges, and areas with abundant blueberry shrubs throughout New England's temperate regions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many bee species, Melitta americana is a specialist pollinator that has evolved a strong preference for blueberry flowers, making it a crucial pollinator for both wild blueberry reproduction and commercial blueberry cultivation.",
